<!-- [HUDS BUTTON 2026-11-10] Wolf HUDs (wolffloaterhuds.cpp), opened by the labelled HUDs button at the bottom right
     (panel_toolbar_view.xml wolf_huds_button). Paul: "on the bottom left of the screen all those tiny buttons look messy -
     have a Huds button in the bottom right control that opens a colourful floater with each different hud as an
     illustrated button".
     Who and why: a resident (35-65, perhaps with weaker eyesight) wants the flight deck or the game HUD without hunting for
     a 22 px icon. Flow: press HUDs, see a big picture, a plain name, one line saying what it is and ON / OFF for every HUD;
     press one: it shows or hides exactly as its old tiny icon did; switching one ON closes this window so the HUD can be
     seen. Each tile is ONE button (the whole tile, so the name and words are pressable too: the picture and words on it
     are mouse_opaque="false" and let the press through, llview.cpp childrenHandleMouseEvent / blockMouseEvent); Tab moves
     between tiles, Enter or Space presses. Pictures: our own artwork, textures/icons/wolf_huds_<id>.png
     (region-terrain-work/huds-button-2026-11-10/make_hud_art.py), the same as WolfStorm's icons/huds/<id>.svg.
     Same tiles, order and words as WolfStorm floater_wolf_huds.js (which has the HUDs WolfStorm has).
     [W104 FEATFIX 2026-11-11] Paul: "greedy the hud isn't on the hud menu, I think there are a lot of huds not on the floater
     that should be": rows 4-5 add Greedy, Roleplay, Tessa's Compass and WolfSpeed (pictures: region-terrain-work/
     w104-review-2026-11-11/featfix/make_hud_art_extra.py). WolfStorm has not had them added yet. -->
<floater
 can_close="true"
 can_minimize="true"
 can_resize="false"
 height="950"
 layout="topleft"
 name="wolf_huds"
 save_rect="true"
 single_instance="true"
 title="Wolf HUDs"
 width="824">
    <text follows="left|top" font="SansSerifLarge" height="28" layout="topleft" left="12" name="intro" top="30" width="784" wrap="true">Press a picture to show or hide that HUD. A green dot and ON mean it is showing.</text>
    <!-- [T958 2026-11-21] ticket 958, Luke Koda: "hide buttons on the HUD that I dont use... pick and choose". Choose tiles... turns
         choosing on (a press then shows or hides a tile here instead of running its HUD; hidden ones are dimmed and say so),
         Done turns it off and the hidden tiles go, the rest closing up. Show all brings every tile back. The choice is the
         resident's own (settings_per_account.xml WolfHudsHidden). Hidden tiles stay in the Wolf World menu, and a HUD keeps
         running when its tile is hidden. wolffloaterhuds.cpp fitToArea keeps this row under the intro however it wraps. -->
    <button follows="left|top" height="24" label="Choose tiles..." label_selected="Done" layout="topleft" left="12" name="choose_btn" top="64" width="150" is_toggle="true"
     tool_tip="Pick which tiles show in this window. Hidden ones stay in the Wolf World menu, and their HUDs still work." />
    <button follows="left|top" height="24" label="Show all" layout="topleft" left="170" name="show_all_btn" top="64" width="100" visible="false"
     tool_tip="Show every tile in this window again" />
    <!-- [HUDS TILES 2 2026-11-14] the tiles scroll. Seven rows (804 high) no longer fit every screen: 920 tall at UI size 1.0
         is more than the floaters' area of a 1080-high screen at a larger UI size (llfloater.cpp LLFloaterView::adjustToFitScreen
         only shrinks resizable floaters, and this one is not). So the tiles sit in tiles_panel inside tiles_scroll, and
         wolffloaterhuds.cpp fitToArea makes the window no taller than the floaters' area (LLFloaterView::getSnapRect, in UI
         units, so it follows the UI size), the tiles scrolling inside. Panel coordinates are the old floater ones less 64
         (the scroll's top). The window is 16 wider (824) so the vertical scroll bar (settings.xml UIScrollbarSize 15) never
         covers a tile nor brings a horizontal one (tiles_panel is 796 wide, the right edge of the last column; 824 less a bar of
         up to 28 still leaves it room).
         [HUDS SMALL 2026-11-14] This XML is the three-column layout. In a floaters' area narrower than 824 (Paul's viewer window
         made small: 640 pixels wide, 474 UI units at UI size 1.35) wolffloaterhuds.cpp fitToArea lays the same tiles out two or
         one to a row (560 / 296 wide) in this reading order, each picture and its words kept at these offsets from its tile, and
         the intro and status line wrap to the narrower width. A new tile still goes in the next free slot of this 3-wide grid. -->
    <scroll_container follows="all" layout="topleft" left="0" top="94" width="824" height="804" name="tiles_scroll" opaque="false" border_visible="false" reserve_scroll_corner="false">
